VALIDATED

## What is proven

Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, the exact `fizz_buzz` closure generated
from `solution.py` is partially correct for every K `Int` input. If the call
terminates, it returns the number of decimal digit `7`s occurring in the
nonnegative integers below `n` that are divisible by 11 or 13. For `n <= 0`,
the result is zero, matching Python's empty `range(n)`.

The theorem observes the returned integer and also constrains the call to
restore the module environment, scope allocator, empty heap, empty stack,
return state, exception state, and exit code shown in the entry claim.
Temporary locals are deallocated by the supplied call semantics.

This is a partial-correctness result, as defined by the Kit workflow. A
separate well-founded argument supports termination: the outer loop decreases
a positive `i` by one, and the inner loop replaces a positive `x` by
`x // 10`, which is strictly smaller and nonnegative.

## Formal claim

`SPEC.fizz-buzz` starts from `Call(Name("fizz_buzz"), Int(N))` with
`"fizz_buzz"` bound to the exact closure body in `solution.mpy`. It reaches an
integer `R` satisfying:

```text
R = fizzResult(0, N)
```

The mathematical summaries are accumulator recurrences. Let
`b(x) = 1` when `x mod 10 = 7`, and `0` otherwise, and let
`q(x) = x // 10` for positive `x`.

```text
digitResult(c, x) = c                                      when x <= 0
digitResult(c, x) = digitResult(c + b(x), q(x))            when x > 0

fizzResult(c, n) = c                                       when n <= 0
fizzResult(c, n) =
  fizzResult(digitResult(c, n - 1), n - 1)                 when n > 0
    if (n - 1) is divisible by 11 or 13
  fizzResult(c, n - 1)                                     otherwise
```

The equations in `verification.k` are oriented as folds so symbolic calls do
not unfold forever. Their orientation changes proof search, not their
mathematical meaning. `SPEC.inner-loop` proves the first recurrence against
the real inner `#while`; `SPEC.outer-loop` proves the second and uses the
inner claim; the entry claim executes lookup, argument evaluation, call-frame
creation, the exact function body, return, and frame removal.

## Proof-extension inventory

There are no operational bridges, opaque result oracles, trusted primitives,
priority rules, or rules that intercept a program AST term.

### `digitResult`

| Extension | Class and role | Domain and equation audit | Context, state, and value | Justification and dependents | Validation |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `digitResult(C,N) => C` | Definitional summary; does not replace execution | `N <= 0`; base case is disjoint from the positive cases | No configuration context or state footprint; fixes the inner-loop result | An exhausted/non-starting digit loop preserves `C`; used by `inner-loop`, `outer-loop`, and `fizz-buzz` | Full proof `#Top`; concrete nonpositive cases pass |
| One-digit boundary equation at `verification.k`'s second `digitResult` rule | Derived definitional equation | `N > 0` and `(N - pyMod(N,10))/10 <= 0`; returns `C + b(N)` | No configuration context or state; result-bearing | Consequence of one real loop iteration followed by the base case; used to close the terminal inner branch | Guards do not overlap the nonpositive base; full proof `#Top` |
| Fold from `digitResult(C + b(N), q(N))` to `digitResult(C,N)` | Definitional recurrence | `N > 0`; the left side inlines the supplied `pyMod` normal form so matching is structural | No operational context or state; value affects both loop summaries and the final result | Exact decimal digit recurrence; `q(N)` is nonnegative and smaller than positive `N`; used by all three claims | `inner-loop` and full proof print `#Top`; the off-by-one probe is rejected; arithmetic model agrees with an independent string oracle |

The base and positive recurrence cover all integers. The boundary equation
overlaps the recurrence only as a true consequence of it. The fold removes
the exact quotient-shaped proof term, so rewrite use terminates on the states
generated by the proof.

### `fizzResult`

| Extension | Class and role | Domain and equation audit | Context, state, and value | Justification and dependents | Validation |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `fizzResult(C,N) => C` | Definitional summary | `N <= 0` | No configuration context or state; fixes the result for empty `range(N)` | Python `range(N)` is empty for nonpositive `N`; used by `outer-loop` and `fizz-buzz` | Negative and zero concrete cases pass; full proof `#Top` |
| Qualifying fold | Definitional recurrence | `N > 0` and normalized `(N-1) mod 11 = 0` or `(N-1) mod 13 = 0` | No operational context or state; incorporates `digitResult(C,N-1)` | Exact contribution of the next candidate in the countdown; used by `outer-loop` and `fizz-buzz` | Full proof `#Top`; examples 78 and 79 distinguish values |
| Non-qualifying fold | Definitional recurrence | `N > 0` and the negation of the qualifying guard | No operational context or state; preserves `C` for the skipped candidate | Exact complement of the qualifying case; used by `outer-loop` and `fizz-buzz` | Guard is disjoint from and exhaustive with the qualifying fold; full proof `#Top` |

The base and two positive folds cover all integers. The two positive guards
are Boolean complements, so their equations cannot disagree on an overlap.
Each fold removes the syntactic `N +Int -1` generated by one verified outer
iteration.

